C(97,'Newton: reading the Principia','history',[21,24,26,29],'principia newton mitmech',
 'Edition and language: 1687 versus later editions;definitions of quantity of motion;geometric style > Keep an edition-and-translation log for the opening definitions | Laws and corollaries: inertia;force as change of motion;composition and action–reaction > Translate each law into modern notation with its assumptions | Book I: central-force geometry;area law;inverse-square orbits > Reconstruct the area-law argument with a polygonal orbit | Book II: resisting media;fluid models;limits of historical hypotheses > Compare one resistance assumption with a modern drag model | Book III and reception: universal gravitation;phenomena and inference;revisions and observational tests > Build a claim-to-observation table for planetary motion',
 'Connect zero central torque to conservation of angular momentum and equal areas in equal times.',
 'Compare one proposition in facsimile, scholarly transcription, and a modern derivation; distinguish translation from interpretation.',
 'Explain how mathematical propositions and empirical premises cooperate in Newton’s gravitational argument.',
 'Newtonian gravity is extraordinarily useful in its domain; neither the original text nor the modern model is exact at every speed and curvature.', 'gravity orbit')
C(98,'Newton: optics, calculus, notebooks, and the broader archive','history',[6,14,49,97],'opticks waste newton chymistry newtonnli',
 'Prisms and color: refrangibility;white-light decomposition;experimental controls > Diagram the two-prism test using ray optics | Opticks beyond the opening: rings;Queries;observation versus conjecture > Label five passages by evidential role | Mathematics in development: infinite series;fluxions;notebook chronology > Compare a modern derivative with a documented fluxional construction | Beyond natural philosophy: theology;chronology;alchemy and Mint administration > Classify archive records without treating them as modern physics results | Archival comparison: diplomatic text;normalized spelling;editorial apparatus > Preserve a quotation’s document identifier, folio, and edition',
 'Use a small-angle prism approximation to explain how wavelength-dependent refractive index separates colors.',
 'Write a source study comparing an optical experiment, mathematical notebook entry, and one non-physics manuscript.',
 'Navigate separate Newton collections and identify what a chosen document supports and what remains interpretation.',
 'A historical notebook recipe is not a modern experimental protocol; Newton’s scientific and non-scientific writings require different forms of evaluation.', 'lens diffraction')
C(99,'Einstein’s 1905 investigations','history',[55,60,75,76],'ein1905 einlight einbrown einsr einmass einbook',
 'Light quanta: heuristic reasoning;photoelectric threshold;frequency versus intensity > Plot stopping potential against frequency | Brownian motion: diffusion;mean-square displacement;molecular evidence > Fit a synthetic displacement ensemble to estimate D | Special relativity: clock synchronization;simultaneity;Lorentz transformations > Compare two observers’ event coordinates | Inertia and energy: rest energy;radiation argument;scope of the short paper > Reconcile the 1905 argument with modern four-momentum | A year in context: dissertation on molecular dimensions;four famous papers;later interpretations > Build a dated reading ledger without calling four papers the entire year’s work',
 'Derive the one-dimensional relation mean-square displacement = 2Dt from the diffusion equation.',
 'Reproduce a Brownian-motion estimate and a Lorentz-coordinate example, annotating original and modern notation.',
 'State the distinct questions, assumptions, and observable predictions of the four famous 1905 papers.',
 'Einstein’s 1905 work was not a single derivation of E = mc², and his light-quantum argument did not constitute all of quantum mechanics.', 'photon relativity')
C(100,'Einstein: general relativity and cosmology','history',[77,78,79,80,81],'eingr eincosmo einstein eincatalog tonggr',
 'Route to field equations: equivalence principle;covariance;geometry versus coordinates > Reconstruct a dated argument map with archival references | Technical exposition: metric;connection;stress-energy and field equations > Translate a short 1916 calculation into modern indices | Classical tests: perihelion shift;light deflection;redshift > Separate predicted quantities from measurement procedures | Cosmological models: static universe;cosmological term;later expanding models > Contrast assumptions of the 1917 model with an FLRW calculation | Waves and global geometry: weak-field waves;coordinate issues;Einstein–Rosen work as a reading route > Locate primary records and compare their claims with a modern textbook',
 'Take the weak-field, slow-motion limit of the metric and relate geodesic acceleration to the Newtonian potential.',
 'Create a figure connecting a primary GR argument to a modern tested observable, including uncertainty and domain of validity.',
 'Explain why general covariance alone does not determine the physical field equations or establish an empirical result.',
 'A cosmological term is part of a model; its historical introduction does not by itself explain the modern dark-energy measurements.', 'schwarzschild')
C(101,'Einstein: quantum theory, EPR, and later unification','history',[57,69,70,74,100],'einradiation einbose epr ein17 eincatalog',
 'Radiation and matter: A and B coefficients;stimulated emission;solid-specific-heat reading route > Recover an equilibrium relation among radiation coefficients | Bose and Einstein: Bose’s counting antecedent;ideal quantum gas;condensation prediction > Compare Bose and Boltzmann occupation at low energy | EPR: completeness criterion;correlated variables;locality assumptions > Write the original argument as explicit premises and conclusion | Bell and later experiments: hidden-variable assumptions;CHSH inequality;no-signalling > Separate EPR’s 1935 argument from a later spin experiment | Unification and correspondence: teleparallel approaches;collaborators and criticism;incomplete later corpus > Follow one exchange across a volume and an archival catalogue',
 'Derive the CHSH bound of 2 for deterministic local outcomes before comparing a quantum singlet prediction.',
 'Write a two-page comparison of EPR, Bell, and an explicitly chosen modern experiment; include what each does not establish.',
 'Distinguish Einstein’s successful contributions from unfinished programs and identify Bose, Podolsky, Rosen, and other collaborators.',
 'Einstein’s concern about quantum completeness was not ignorance of entanglement; his later unified-field efforts did not become a validated completed theory.')
C(102,'Tesla: polyphase power and rotating machinery','history',[38,39,40,41,45],'teslamotor teslapatents tesla mitem',
 'Engineering context: AC generation;transmission constraints;multiple contributors > Draw a system boundary around a polyphase power network | Patent close reading: US381968A;claims versus specification;winding phase relationships > Annotate the exact granted claim and an explanatory drawing | Rotating fields: orthogonal coils;phase shifts;synchronous speed > Sum ideal two-phase fields numerically | Induction machinery: rotor currents;slip;torque and loss mechanisms > Sketch a torque-speed curve and label its idealizations | From invention to system: manufacture;licensing and chronology;measured versus claimed performance > Create an evidence ledger for one engineering achievement',
 'Show that two orthogonal equal-amplitude fields in quadrature produce a constant-magnitude rotating vector.',
 'Build a low-voltage-equivalent simulation of a rotating magnetic field and compare it with the patent’s stated arrangement.',
 'Explain Tesla’s documented polyphase contributions without assigning every part of alternating-current technology to one person.',
 'Tesla did not invent all AC phenomena or every component of power engineering; patents have defined claims and historical context.', 'transformer')
C(103,'Tesla: resonance, radio control, transmission, and the turbine','history',[32,41,43,44,102],'teslatransformer teslacontrol teslapower teslaturbine tesla1892 wardenclyffe',
 'Resonance and coils: coupled oscillators;distributed capacitance;loss and bandwidth > Compare an ideal LC frequency with a lossy RLC response | High-frequency lectures: apparatus descriptions;discharge observations;measurement limitations > Annotate the 1892 lecture without treating every interpretation as established | Remote control: US613809A;receiver-command-actuator chain;teleoperation > Simulate a noisy command channel and a discrete actuator state machine | Wireless ambitions: US645576A;coupling and radiation;Wardenclyffe evidence > Construct an energy-budget critique of a proposed transmission system | Disk turbine and wider writings: US1061206A;boundary-layer momentum exchange;lighting and X-ray records as archive routes > Compare a patent explanation with a modern viscous-flow model',
 'Derive average resistor dissipation and stored energy for a sinusoidal RLC model, then define its quality factor.',
 'Produce a simulation-only resonance report and a patent-claim audit for either wireless transmission or the disk turbine.',
 'Separate documented inventions, working demonstrations, patent proposals, and later unsupported stories.',
 'Wireless transmission transfers supplied energy; resonance does not create energy. Wardenclyffe was not a demonstrated worldwide electricity network.', 'rlc induction transformer')
C(104,'Working through complete-work archives','history',[97,98,99,100,101,102,103],'newton einstein einportal eincatalog tesla teslalectures teslawritings teslapatents',
 'Define the corpus: works;letters;notebooks and editions > State whether your project means published scientific works or all surviving documents | Catalogue methods: identifiers;date ranges;language and provenance > Design a document ledger with stable identifiers and coverage gaps | Obtain a text: facsimile;transcription;translation and access restrictions > Trace one record to the readable object and record any failure | Compare evidence: original claim;editorial context;modern assessment > Audit a famous quotation against the earliest available primary record | Maintain the reading program: topic tags;versioned annotations;unavailable and unprocessed items > Schedule a ten-document sequence rather than claiming to have read an entire archive',
 'Construct a provenance graph linking record, scan, transcription, translation, and your own interpretation.',
 'Complete ten ledger entries across all three scientists, including two unavailable or incomplete items and an honest access note.',
 'Produce a reproducible reading bibliography with explicit inclusion criteria and gaps.',
 'A catalogue count is not a count of scientific papers, inventions, online scans, or verified claims.')
C(105,'Reading research papers and evaluating claims','research',[14,15],'arxivapi nist python',
 'Triage: abstract question;study type;submission and publication status > Classify five papers as theory, simulation, experiment, review, or proposal | Read the model: definitions;approximations;boundary conditions > List every assumption behind one key equation | Inspect evidence: controls;uncertainty;selection and look-elsewhere effects > Rebuild a claim-to-figure matrix | Compare the literature: baselines;contradictions;replication and corrections > Follow references backward and citations forward with a dated log | Explain honestly: supported conclusion;limitations;next discriminating test > Write a 300-word critique with one falsifiable follow-up',
 'Work through a paper’s central equation from its declared assumptions; identify the first step you cannot independently justify.',
 'Complete one worksheet each for an experiment, a theoretical result, and a numerical proposal.',
 'Explain what the paper actually measured or proved, what it assumed, and what additional evidence would change your assessment.',
 'A preprint is a research communication, not independent validation; peer review also does not eliminate uncertainty or guarantee reproducibility.')
C(106,'Computational research, reproducibility, and HPC','research',[11,14,15],'scipy sympy mitnum python missing',
 'Reproducible environments: package versions;random seeds;data provenance > Produce a minimal environment and deterministic small example | Numerical reliability: consistency;stability;convergence and conditioning > Separate truncation, iteration, and floating-point errors | Scientific software: unit-aware interfaces;analytic limits;regression benchmarks > Test a physical conservation law and a mesh-refinement trend | Scale carefully: profiling;vectorization;parallel decomposition and I/O > Profile first, then report time and memory for one justified optimization | Publish artifacts: configurations;uncertainty-aware figures;licensing and hardware records > Rebuild a figure from a clean directory with a single command',
 'Estimate observed convergence order from three resolutions and explain when Richardson extrapolation is justified.',
 'Reproduce one figure using an established solver, pin inputs, and compare computational cost at matched error.',
 'Deliver a repository that another person can run and audit, including negative results and numerical limitations.',
 'A fast simulation or a visually convincing animation is not evidence of convergence, physical fidelity, or a new discovery.')
C(107,'Experiments, inverse problems, and statistical inference','research',[14,15,19],'mitnum nist scipy',
 'Design: estimand;control variables;calibration chain > Write a measurement equation before collecting data | Noise and systematic effects: resolution;drift;correlated errors > Simulate repeatability and calibration bias separately | Inference: likelihood;priors;identifiability > Fit a noisy decay and inspect parameter degeneracy | Model comparison: held-out checks;residual structure;sensitivity analysis > Test an alternative model using a declared decision rule | Report: uncertainty budget;replication protocol;ethical and practical limits > Produce a result with units, uncertainty, and a bounded conclusion',
 'Derive the weighted least-squares estimator for a linear model under independent Gaussian errors, then state how correlation changes it.',
 'Recover a known parameter from blinded synthetic data and test whether interval coverage matches the declared confidence procedure.',
 'Distinguish statistical precision from accuracy and explain which uncertainties dominate your conclusion.',
 'A small fit residual does not guarantee an identifiable model; statistical significance is not effect size or physical importance.', 'decay')
C(108,'From replication to a frontier proposal','research',[105,106,107],'arxivapi missing',
 'Choose a tractable question: prerequisites;existing baselines;available data and hardware > Scope a one-figure replication with a two-week budget | Reproduce first: original settings;documented deviations;failed attempts > Record a baseline before trying improvements | Extend one axis: parameter regime;measurement precision;alternative explanation > Change one scientifically motivated factor at a time | Seek criticism: readable notes;issue reports;seminar discussion > Prepare a five-minute methods-focused presentation | Write the proposal: hypothesis;discriminating test;risks and stopping rules > Draft a proposal with a null-result publication plan',
 'Translate your hypothesis into a predicted observable and a calculation or measurement that could reject it.',
 'Submit a reproducible replication package and a 1,500-word proposal identifying one unresolved, bounded question.',
 'Demonstrate prerequisite mastery, a reproducible baseline, and a falsifiable extension reviewed by a knowledgeable peer.',
 'Finishing a roadmap does not mean mastering all physics or being ready to resolve quantum gravity; research competence develops within a specific domain.')
